#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049872 :topic/difficulty 0.8548075996646627 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428573 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2062->1252. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050491} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428574 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2062->3540. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050172} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "19ee4c58-94d6-55c3-b606-10cb798ebeba" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129121 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128877 :question/id #uuid "e2e5002a-d3ae-5831-b405-41618960aee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15609" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = -2\\sin\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right) + 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)= A\\sin(Bx +C) + D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-2.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129122 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128878 :question/id #uuid "7f6626c7-c3d3-5f4e-b2c7-1e9bf07e883c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15610" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\cos(2x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y = A\\cos(Bx +C) + D,$ the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2.$ Therefore, the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\pi.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129123 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128880 :question/id #uuid "bfa446cd-c215-59c2-b84d-35d7897e9e34" :question/math-academy-id "e-15615" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-6$ and a maximum value of $8.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{y_{\\textrm{min}}+y_{\\textrm{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-6+8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128879 :question/id #uuid "61b6821f-3ee5-5dd8-b8a3-ee1859645237" :question/math-academy-id "e-15611" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x) = -4\\cos(3x)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\cos x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\leq\\cos\\left(3x \\right) \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\le 1 \\\\ 4 & \\ge -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\ge -4 \\\\ 2 & \\ge -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2\\ge -6 \\\\ -6 & \\le f(x)\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-6,2].$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5696c2d5-4e35-5ed3-a4a2-6fe9b158e461" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2062 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050592}] :topic/title "Properties of Transformed Sine and Cosine Functions"}]}
